 good  and bad taliban that is also a very um  you know it's  it's a policy that uh has brought a lot  of harm to pakistan right we want to  talk about that and  you talked about the policy in terms of  a suicidal policy and also the policy of  strategic depth i'd like to pick them up  one at a time what exactly does pakistan  mean by strategic depth  well  you see pakistan has been working on  having a  strategic depth in afghanistan as far as  the region is concerned and  uh used it as  uh you know  to counter india's  uh interests also and the  since 47 the uh  the  the friction that the two countries have  now i think that this uh policy hasn't  worked and it has actually brought a lot  of uh  trouble  for the region and for pakistan and  especially for the bush students in the  tribal  areas  right and that brings me to the word  suicidal why would you say this policy  has been suicidal for pakistan  well look what where we are right now  i mean our homes have been  destroyed uh  the threats  to country the existential  threats to the country so it is suicidal  it has brought more harm to the country  rather than and it is also i mean for  the whole region it's a  policy that has  really not benefited the country how can  investing in terrorists really benefit  a country  so personally i feel that  we need to  address the problems in the region  with our neighbors without really  working through proxies  and these proxies are really i mean  there's no good bad taliban  in my opinion and  we  and  this policy because i i mean the entire  both  has been affected i was recently in  quetta and i found a  taliban  freely moving around  in the  prop  in the city this is something that we  need to question i mean  terrorists  can move around freely whereas  uh citizens who are questioning these  policies are under great threat  if i can just ask you we have about a  minute left what do you think pakistan's  policy will be now given that the  taliban are running afghanistan  well in my opinion  taliban in afghanistan and  taliban are terrorists and they are  occupying afghanistan with the support  of  certain countries and the u.s the  doha deal  gave these  terrorists legitimacy and i feel that  it's going to affect us it's going to  hit us hard  right now uh the the strategic depth  that pakistan has been working for  they might have  gotten it for  a little while but it's going to hit us  back  we'll leave it there for the time being  but thank you so much for joining us  today bushra gohar former member of  pakistan's parliament thank you so much  ma'am  let's bring in dws amrita chima our  what's the view there in india of the  recent developments in afghanistan  well  certainly none of the euphoria that you  saw in parts of pakistan pointed the  contrary there was huge alarm because  the indian government had very good  relations with successive afghan  governments over the past 20 years and  it also invested a huge amount in  afghanistan in building infrastructure  and also a hugely symbolic gesture  building the new parliament in the  country and now the taliban have  announced their cabinet and 17 out of  the 33 people in the cabinet are  people who are designated as terrorists  by the u.n and many of them are linked  to groups that india feels uh were  responsible for targeting indian indian  facilities for example the consulates in  afghanistan which were bombed by these  some of these groups so india is not at  all happy about the situation there and  it's also very worried that the interior  minister for example is linked to a  deadly group operating in pakistan the  so-called haqqani group so they are  valid at multiple levels  so what are india's other concerns then  apart from the fact that the people of  pakistan they are worried about that all  the gains made in the past 20 years for  minority rights women's rights and  democratic institutions will be rolled  back they are worried about security  because you have multiple factions  operating in afghanistan at the moment  and this is fertile ground for terrorist  organizations like the al-qaeda like the  islamic state to flourish and this will  have a destabilizing influence not just  in afghanistan but neighboring countries  like pakistan the neighbors of  afghanistan and central asia and india  is particularly worried that there will  be a spill over into its northern  territory of kashmir where it's been  fighting a separatist monitors movement  for decades  i mean as a  substantial regional power what are  india's options there  you know fairly limited to be honest at  the moment gerhard because at the moment  pakistan seems to be calling all the  shots in afghanistan and in fact some  people here feel that it's a proxy  pakistani government that you have uh in  afghanistan but india is weighing its  options it has lost a lot of diplomatic  leverage but it's working with  international organizations they've been  several high-level  visits in india from for example  intelligence chiefs from the us and  russia  and india is still very keen that  humanitarian aid should go into  afghanistan even if they do not  recognize the taliban so prime minister  modi is going to the united states next  week he'll be having a bilateral chat  with president joe biden there and  afghanistan and how to deal with  afghanistan will definitely be top of  the agenda  dws amrita there in delhi thank you amir

